#edb/ref 17592186270150} {:answer/id #uuid "639d7aa9-6e9c-56a5-93f9-c52aa4f843af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270151} {:answer/id #uuid "65c55cd3-8c13-52a3-a22c-496d7ed37306" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270176} {:answer/id #uuid "76ca705e-0bfb-57d2-b037-21e60eec8ae8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270317} {:answer/id #uuid "952621dd-e2c2-5a13-b19f-0690051a27fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270575}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "952621dd-e2c2-5a13-b19f-0690051a27fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270575} :answer-field/id #uuid "499365bc-c9cb-5bfb-b557-42f54e59d06b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271642}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "42cc7718-b801-5574-add3-311d9f7972bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-141111" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-2y\\operatorname{tan}⁡2x,\\,y(\\frac{\\pi }{8})=\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nYou're given that $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x$ is the solution to this initial value problem. What is the value of the constant $A?$" :question/worked-solution "We're given that $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x$ is the solution to the initial value problem. Therefore, it must satisfy the differential equation *and* the initial condition $y(\\frac{\\pi }{8})=\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nSubstituting the initial condition $y(\\frac{\\pi }{8})=\\sqrt{2}$ into our solution and solving for $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & =A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{8}) \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & =A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & =A(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\ A & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272215 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ba2e4d0-14d3-5e3f-a154-f23244402132" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269372} {:answer/id #uuid "2eae9f08-f9ab-5840-be4a-f20ee77083b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269689} {:answer/id #uuid "72af3f51-6c6e-5bd3-b3d5-192c13e81d79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270282} {:answer/id #uuid "d2a550bb-d2ce-54f1-bcd0-9c271a541331" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271124} {:answer/id #uuid "f528aba0-decc-5d96-86c5-93d306efde35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271418}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2eae9f08-f9ab-5840-be4a-f20ee77083b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269689} :answer-field/id #uuid "59599c50-b345-5a48-9462-d538ac1bac80" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271671}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9915548e-f09c-5476-a595-046194f74c54" :question/math-academy-id "q-141114" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{y}{x},\\,y(-1)=3.\n$$\n\nYou're given that $y=Ax$ is the solution to this initial value problem. What is the value of the constant $A?$" :question/worked-solution "We're given that $y=Ax$ is the solution to the initial value problem. Therefore, it must satisfy the differential equation *and* the initial condition $y(-1)=3.$\n\nSubstituting the initial condition $y(-1)=3$ into our solution and solving for $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =Ax \\\\ 3 & =A(-1) \\\\ 3 & =-A \\\\ A & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=-3x.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319202 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "57db562f-825a-5c06-9c18-a4185051c072" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317861}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "57db562f-825a-5c06-9c18-a4185051c072" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317861} :answer-field/id #uuid "5672edce-69fb-5725-aa5f-85f880ba3571" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318610}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "95cfa877-83ba-5877-86e4-7c000e0d1f2a" :question/math-academy-id "q-347839" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=3y\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x),\\,y\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=1.\n$$\n\nYou are given that $y=A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)$ is the solution to this initial value problem. What is the value of the constant $A?$\n\n$A=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We are given that $y=A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)$ is the solution to the initial value problem. Therefore, it must satisfy the differential equation *and* the initial condition $y\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=1.$\n\nSubstituting the initial condition $y\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=1$ into the solution and solving for $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x) \\\\ 1 & =Asin\\,(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\ 1 & =Asin\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\ 1 & =A(1) \\\\ A & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321016 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9c73f686-0fde-551d-8a04-19d55648ae19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319861}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9c73f686-0fde-551d-8a04-19d55648ae19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319861} :answer-field/id #uuid "1853bdf6-795c-54d2-8aca-6ddb48b65a90" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320219}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1c813122-41f5-5f7d-ad1c-44bd6d1ad9e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-352649" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-2y\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x),\\,y\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{8})=\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nYou are given that $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)$ is the solution to this initial value problem. What is the value of the constant $A?$\n\n$A=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We are given that $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)$ is the solution to the initial value problem. Therefore, it must satisfy the differential equation *and* the initial condition $y\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{8})=\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nSubstituting the initial condition $y\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{8})=\\sqrt{2}$ into the solution and solving for $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & =Acos\\,(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{8}) \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & =A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & =A\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ A & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321017 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24dfcc68-5dea-50bf-b7a8-8e83148e7990" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5/{e}^{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319505}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "24dfcc68-5dea-50bf-b7a8-8e83148e7990" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5/{e}^{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319505} :answer-field/id #uuid "d41db8f8-1e2b-52a4-95d9-3d2f841c0f19" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320601}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "33e8fcf6-acf7-5f2b-b8b8-b3631e5ba43c" :question/math-academy-id "q-352654" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=3y,\\,y(2)=5.\n$$\n\nYou're given that $y=A{e}^{3x}$ is the solution to this initial value problem. What is the value of the constant $A?$\n\n$A=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We're given that $y=A{e}^{3x}$ is the solution to the initial value problem. Therefore, it must satisfy the differential equation *and* the initial condition $y(2)=5.$\n\nSubstituting the initial condition $y(2)=5$ into our solution and solving for $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =A{e}^{3x} \\\\ 5 & =A{e}^{3\\cdot 2} \\\\ 5 & =A{e}^{6} \\\\ \\frac{5}{{e}^{6}} & =A \\\\ A & =\\frac{5}{{e}^{6}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321018 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36f58e9f-d185-562c-9c67-7e75f87623d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319560}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "36f58e9f-d185-562c-9c67-7e75f87623d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319560} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a793135-2916-5a04-8d25-0d156d312364" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320388}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a255cc3f-5f22-5919-beaf-e4725720aff5" :question/math-academy-id "q-352655" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-4y,\\,y(1)=2.\n$$\n\nYou're given that $y=A{e}^{-4x}$ is the solution to this initial value problem. What is the value of the constant $A?$\n\n$A=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We're given that $y=A{e}^{-4x}$ is the solution to the initial value problem. Therefore, it must satisfy the differential equation *and* the initial condition $y(1)=2.$\n\nSubstituting the initial condition $y(1)=2$ into our solution and solving for $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =A{e}^{-4x} \\\\ 2 & =A{e}^{-4\\cdot 1} \\\\ 2 & =A{e}^{-4} \\\\ 2{e}^{4} & =A \\\\ A & =2{e}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321019 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f8e7e8f-73cf-53bb-83a4-6766a894f35d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319822}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f8e7e8f-73cf-53bb-83a4-6766a894f35d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319822} :answer-field/id #uuid "66f2860e-a104-57a8-b392-d8993df50cb7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320376}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "50e3aacc-64ad-599b-9fdb-eea729801f82" :question/math-academy-id "q-352656" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=y,\\,y(-1)=4.\n$$\n\nYou're given that $y=A{e}^{x}$ is the solution to this initial value problem. What is the value of the constant $A?$\n\n$A=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We're given that $y=A{e}^{x}$ is the solution to the initial value problem. Therefore, it must satisfy the differential equation *and* the initial condition $y(-1)=4.$\n\nSubstituting the initial condition $y(-1)=4$ into our solution and solving for $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =A{e}^{x} \\\\ 4 & =A{e}^{-1} \\\\ 4 & =A{e}^{-1} \\\\ 4e & =A \\\\ A & =4e.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321021 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f8acbb56-9fb6-58fa-a74e-030faab46fc8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320132}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f8acbb56-9fb6-58fa-a74e-030faab46fc8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320132} :answer-field/id #uuid "c34d4846-12a9-583c-9e1f-8c59dd074ddb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320567}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2f863bf4-17d0-5519-90b1-53b7529243b1" :question/math-academy-id "q-352660" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{4y}{x},\\,y(2)=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nYou're given that $y=\\frac{A}{{x}^{4}}$ is the solution to this initial value problem. What is the value of the constant $A?$\n\n$A=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We're given that $y=\\frac{A}{{x}^{4}}$ is the solution to the initial value problem. Therefore, it must satisfy the differential equation *and* the initial condition $y(2)=\\frac{3}{4}.$\n\nSubstituting the initial condition $y(2)=\\frac{3}{4}$ into our solution and solving for $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{A}{{x}^{4}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{4} & =\\frac{A}{{2}^{4}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{4} & =\\frac{A}{16} \\\\ A & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321022 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fc24bb63-d5ea-5647-8e74-82ebea88b815" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1/3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320150}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fc24bb63-d5ea-5647-8e74-82ebea88b815" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1/3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320150} :answer-field/id #uuid "bbae27ae-1c5d-5215-9548-779ff9205ad0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320549}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "baa48ee7-6d45-5298-b9b3-7a1a62413c86" :question/math-academy-id "q-352661" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={y}^{2},\\,y(0)=3.\n$$\n\nYou're given that $y=\\frac{1}{A-x}$ is the solution to this initial value problem. What is the value of the constant $A?$\n\n$A=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We're given that $y=\\frac{1}{A-x}$ is the solution to the initial value problem. Therefore, it must satisfy the differential equation *and* the initial condition $y(0)=3.$\n\nSubstituting the initial condition $y(0)=3$ into our solution and solving for $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{A-x} \\\\ 3 & =\\frac{1}{A-0} \\\\ 3 & =\\frac{1}{A} \\\\ A & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321023 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "303cc3dd-ed53-5fc3-801d-bfcb237bc936" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1/2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319544}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "303cc3dd-ed53-5fc3-801d-bfcb237bc936" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1/2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319544} :answer-field/id #uuid "f83209fb-2c55-58c7-b40d-019c31ec1356" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320690}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "44f980f1-e507-5172-bd9f-13aac1021e42" :question/math-academy-id "q-352663" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{3y}{x},\\,y(2)=4.\n$$\n\nYou're given that $y=A{x}^{3}$ is the solution to this initial value problem. What is the value of the constant $A?$\n\n$A=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We're given that $y=A{x}^{3}$ is the solution to the initial value problem. Therefore, it must satisfy the differential equation *and* the initial condition $y(2)=4.$\n\nSubstituting the initial condition $y(2)=4$ into our solution and solving for $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =A{x}^{3} \\\\ 4 & =A(2)^{3} \\\\ 4 & =A(8) \\\\ A & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373785 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60b15d99-5f73-5d71-a0a0-49f9be9df65d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373787} {:answer/id #uuid "3cefcde2-3aa5-51a2-8f0c-24c6172e631c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373788} {:answer/id #uuid "243ba24f-c088-5741-b1dc-797cc0c88bb6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373789} {:answer/id #uuid "3ab4b002-dd36-598f-8102-522b7e368735" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373790} {:answer/id #uuid "4a73df82-8e85-5bb1-90fc-89e5be58e836" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373791}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3cefcde2-3aa5-51a2-8f0c-24c6172e631c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373788} :answer-field/id #uuid "e02b7c91-76ce-53ed-b342-163951d05f8f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373786}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8abc10be-3eb5-5e2c-9a94-244a431b287a" :question/math-academy-id "q-141112" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{y}{x},\\,y(-2)=-1.\n$$\n\nYou're given that $y=\\frac{A}{x}$ is the solution to this initial value problem. What is the value of the constant $A?$" :question/worked-solution "We're given that $y=\\frac{A}{x}$ is the solution to the initial value problem. Therefore, it must satisfy the differential equation *and* the initial condition $y(-2)=-1.$\n\nSubstituting the initial condition $y(-2)=-1$ into our solution and solving for $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{A}{x} \\\\ -1 & =\\frac{A}{(-2)} \\\\ A & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{2}{x}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398445 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "024ac53a-9f0a-5a7a-98f8-c50bcc20b4b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398447} {:answer/id #uuid "6125d774-7a34-522e-a338-4e504b088a07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398448} {:answer/id #uuid "5be53b7e-12b8-56ea-82fa-df826cb81389" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398449} {:answer/id #uuid "43ee5f6e-6b96-5920-96dd-6bda4e98e633" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398450} {:answer/id #uuid "6ffa9645-4799-55ea-b858-499c63ccbd0f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398451}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5be53b7e-12b8-56ea-82fa-df826cb81389" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398449} :answer-field/id #uuid "cdc013cf-89dd-52e2-93ec-1bd8584646b6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398446}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0e0065e9-3a69-5f85-ad17-2869bffc442f" :question/math-academy-id "q-141113" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{2y}{x},\\,y(2)=-1.\n$$\n\nYou're given that $y=\\frac{A}{{x}^{2}}$ is the solution to this initial value problem. What is the value of the constant $A?$" :question/worked-solution "We're given that $y=\\frac{A}{{x}^{2}}$ is the solution to the initial value problem. Therefore, it must satisfy the differential equation *and* the initial condition $y(2)=-1.$\n\nSubstituting the initial condition $y(2)=-1$ into our solution and solving for $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{A}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ -1 & =\\frac{A}{{2}^{2}} \\\\ -1 & =\\frac{A}{4} \\\\ A & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398452 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "89027aec-d449-5dca-8830-9b39f9c5fbba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398454} {:answer/id #uuid "d1591666-636c-5480-9f22-08f9eeec3613" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398455} {:answer/id #uuid "b092bcb2-de91-5a2a-9a1c-b1cf1cdf0b26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398456} {:answer/id #uuid "4f0a5d44-77c5-5b01-9db7-d212e6e5f858" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398457} {:answer/id #uuid "408ad86d-b3e2-50fe-ba49-d6d10e8b89e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398458}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4f0a5d44-77c5-5b01-9db7-d212e6e5f858" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398457} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f0f43ae-19bf-5c26-8ca2-6e01020ad358" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398453}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e96414b2-2fe9-598e-bc15-f2c64a7b0633" :question/math-academy-id "q-141115" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{-y},\\,y(1)=1.\n$$\n\nYou're given that $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(A+x)$ is the solution to this initial value problem. What is the value of the constant $A?$" :question/worked-solution "We're given that $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(A+x)$ is the solution to the initial value problem. Therefore, it must satisfy the differential equation *and* the initial condition $y(1)=1.$\n\nSubstituting the initial condition $y(1)=1$ into our solution and solving for $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(A+x) \\\\ 1 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(A+1) \\\\ {e}^{1} & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(A+1)} \\\\ e & =A+1 \\\\ A & =e-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e-1+x).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398459 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2dcefcd7-21a2-550b-82e1-cdf9e8efcbda" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398461} {:answer/id #uuid "4b4230d9-c571-50ea-9f01-98df054d2c1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398462} {:answer/id #uuid "d47c7e95-9d5a-515c-9902-a9d88176c6d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398463} {:answer/id #uuid "89f9fe65-61ad-531c-91e5-0d74f2e05f24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398464} {:answer/id #uuid "86af0317-94e9-53ec-8661-5ce6b18962d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398465}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d47c7e95-9d5a-515c-9902-a9d88176c6d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398463} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f0c471e-3bc9-5406-b7a8-dfcd6f763630" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398460}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ea6ff592-81a4-59f3-84d6-c97bdc2e3a4f" :question/math-academy-id "q-141116" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-{y}^{2},\\,y(0)=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nYou're given that $y=\\frac{1}{A+x}$ is the solution to this initial value problem. What is the value of the constant $A?$" :question/worked-solution "We're given that $y=\\frac{1}{A+x}$ is the solution to the initial value problem. Therefore, it must satisfy the differential equation *and* the initial condition $y(0)=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nSubstituting the initial condition $y(0)=\\frac{1}{2}$ into our solution and solving for $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{A+x} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{1}{A+0} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{1}{A} \\\\ A & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{2+x}.\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to Initial Value Problems"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100959 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100801 :question/id #uuid "1228900d-87d4-5ff0-8e01-ff9417e82220" :question/math-academy-id "e-9978" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = y.\n$$\n\n Which of the following is a solution to this equation?\n\n- $y = e^x + 1$\n\n- $y = -e^x$\n\n- $y = 2e^x$" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWe check each proposed solution by differentiating and substituting it into the original equation. \n\n- Differentiating $y = e^x + 1,$ we get $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = e^x.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y \\\\ {e}^{x} & \\overset{?}{=}{e}^{x}+1 \\\\ 0 & \\ne 1.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *false*. Therefore, the function $y = e^x+1$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y = -e^x,$ we get $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = -e^x.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y \\\\ -{e}^{x} & =-{e}^{x}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y = -e^x$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y = 2e^x,$ we get $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = 2e^x.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y \\\\ 2{e}^{x} & =2{e}^{x}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y = 2e^x$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "80ed79a9-8e39-5c84-9c87-dec266060afc" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101158 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a10cc07-3e1c-5a71-bd0e-71421dba782d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270071} {:answer/id #uuid "6e370130-2378-5cca-9684-975908b5877b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270249} {:answer/id #uuid "99f82b0b-4304-51f8-928d-49d4c5dc2668" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270622} {:answer/id #uuid "9c311079-8a77-5514-87ce-fc3c5ef3f0b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270646} {:answer/id #uuid "f70dbee7-936f-5d25-9f4c-eeba5332fed0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271437}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9c311079-8a77-5514-87ce-fc3c5ef3f0b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270646} :answer-field/id #uuid "365c75b6-595b-5005-a9c8-57ce7634fca8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271616}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e22d897d-f7ce-5b4f-b6d4-aa4c45c66b5a" :question/math-academy-id "q-141096" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-2y.\n$$\n\n Which of the following is a solution to this equation?\n\n- $y=-2{e}^{-2x}$\n- $y=-2{e}^{x}$\n- $y=2{e}^{-x}+1$" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWe check each proposed solution by differentiating and substituting it into the original equation. \n\n- Differentiating $y=-2{e}^{-2x},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=4{e}^{-2x}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-2y \\\\ 4{e}^{-2x} & \\overset{?}{=}-2(-2{e}^{-2x}) \\\\ 4{e}^{-2x} & =4{e}^{-2x}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y=-2{e}^{-2x}$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=-2{e}^{x},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-2{e}^{x}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-2y \\\\ -2{e}^{x} & \\overset{?}{=}-2(-2{e}^{x}) \\\\ -2{e}^{x} & \\overset{?}{=}4{e}^{x} \\\\ 0 & \\ne 6{e}^{x}.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=-2{e}^{x}$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=2{e}^{-x}+1,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-2{e}^{-x}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-2y \\\\ -2{e}^{-x} & \\overset{?}{=}-2(2{e}^{-x}+1) \\\\ -2{e}^{-x} & \\overset{?}{=}-4{e}^{-x}-2 \\\\ 2{e}^{-x} & \\ne -2.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=2{e}^{-x}+1$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101538 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b6d38a0-2a35-516f-a1d9-77c57ce00963" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345438} {:answer/id #uuid "404e89ee-49ff-5b0f-abcd-7d5e0d85fb92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345903} {:answer/id #uuid "947521d6-d028-5c2b-980f-753bc6040fca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346649} {:answer/id #uuid "b081be03-28c5-5fbd-9579-cb5742e049af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346892} {:answer/id #uuid "c7348c4a-0df4-5f20-93b7-2580a8f6a31f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347083}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b081be03-28c5-5fbd-9579-cb5742e049af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346892} :answer-field/id #uuid "3e56ae48-c581-52d9-a963-7da4ef4a6b06" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347709}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a773fc2b-812d-5f5c-8cbb-9b1672c48928" :question/math-academy-id "q-79335" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2y}{x}-2,\\,x\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n Which of the following is a solution to this equation?\n\n- $y=2x$\n- $y={x}^{2}$\n- $y={x}^{3}$" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWe check each proposed solution by differentiating and substituting it into the original equation. \n\n- Differentiating $y=2x,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2y}{x}-2 \\\\ 2 & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2\\cdot 2x}{x}-2 \\\\ 2 & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{4x}{x}-2 \\\\ 2 & \\overset{?}{=}4-2 \\\\ 2 & =2.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y=2x$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y={x}^{2},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2x.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2y}{x}-2 \\\\ 2x & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2\\cdot {x}^{2}}{x}-2 \\\\ 2x & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{x}-2 \\\\ 2x & \\overset{?}{=}2x-2 \\\\ 0 & \\ne -2.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *false*. Therefore, the function $y={x}^{2}$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y={x}^{3},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=3{x}^{2}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2y}{x}-2 \\\\ 3{x}^{2} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2\\cdot {x}^{3}}{x}-2 \\\\ 3{x}^{2} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{x}-2 \\\\ 3{x}^{2} & \\overset{?}{=}2{x}^{2}-2 \\\\ {x}^{2} & \\ne -2\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y={x}^{3}$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272204 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24c381dc-376d-5353-8ba0-92a441ad5120" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269593} {:answer/id #uuid "5934a80e-f359-52ec-958b-93307b322bc0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270058} {:answer/id #uuid "8b75f1c9-bcf0-515e-be7b-9d4e518d6c70" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270477} {:answer/id #uuid "c6bfbf77-64d8-59e2-8500-31531556a0de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271019} {:answer/id #uuid "f61137e8-90ef-5d1f-835b-05645581b298" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271428}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "24c381dc-376d-5353-8ba0-92a441ad5120" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269593} :answer-field/id #uuid "e272136a-ebf1-5cd3-8386-04e9861680b6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271909}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e106766c-2780-56ea-8754-563b11d222dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-141091" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2y\\operatorname{cot}⁡2x.\n$$\n\n Which of the following is a solution to this equation?\n\n- $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x$\n- $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+1$\n- $y=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x$" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWe check each proposed solution by differentiating and substituting it into the original equation. \n\n- Differentiating $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2y\\operatorname{cot}⁡2x \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x & \\overset{?}{=}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\cdot \\operatorname{cot}⁡2x \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+1,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2y\\operatorname{cot}⁡2x \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x & \\overset{?}{=}2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+1)\\operatorname{cot}⁡2x \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x & \\overset{?}{=}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+2\\operatorname{cot}⁡2x \\\\ 0 & =2\\operatorname{cot}⁡2x.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+1$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2y\\operatorname{cot}⁡2x \\\\ -6\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x & \\overset{?}{=}2(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)\\cdot \\operatorname{cot}⁡2x \\\\ -6\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x & =-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272205 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "174e0d49-7a0b-59e7-b52f-4bbee3f180a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269462} {:answer/id #uuid "366949c8-88b6-5e13-a572-ef57d6d34106" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269763} {:answer/id #uuid "97c9d4a8-02fa-5f94-a559-9130ec355a29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270605} {:answer/id #uuid "b4f1d177-eeff-524d-a7d5-855d6a8dde3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270878} {:answer/id #uuid "f6b0cd67-e050-5916-a080-d88d385d894a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271432}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b4f1d177-eeff-524d-a7d5-855d6a8dde3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270878} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e625c3e-5398-5433-bca4-04361125f29a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271791}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e6e3bd06-ef25-5819-bfee-624e8862d278" :question/math-academy-id "q-141092" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-y\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.\n$$\n\n Which of the following is a solution to this equation?\n\n- $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$\n- $y=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$\n- $y=-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWe check each proposed solution by differentiating and substituting it into the original equation. \n\n- Differentiating $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-y\\operatorname{tan}⁡x \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & \\overset{?}{=}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡x \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-y\\operatorname{tan}⁡x \\\\ -3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & \\overset{?}{=}-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡x \\\\ -3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-y\\operatorname{tan}⁡x \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & \\overset{?}{=}-(-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡x \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y=-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272206 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22bc4b9d-c71a-59dc-ae37-22fae65e1789" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269570} {:answer/id #uuid "9c06b791-ad38-5ac6-b6dc-b9a0da4ba7b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270644} {:answer/id #uuid "b8db0ed2-b44d-5a73-a4a9-8538350f5fd9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270903} {:answer/id #uuid "be07e7e3-322f-5d89-89ed-7461cab98f74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270946} {:answer/id #uuid "d3ea9313-1523-5c68-b542-c2a59c465e0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271131}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "be07e7e3-322f-5d89-89ed-7461cab98f74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270946} :answer-field/id #uuid "95f0be9a-434e-5230-83a3-aca01a100695" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271776}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8c4f3444-d6eb-515b-bbe6-1939e7e4a2dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-141093" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2}{{e}^{y/2}}.\n$$\n\n Which of the following is a solution to this equation?\n\n- $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$\n- $y=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$\n- $y=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWe check each proposed solution by differentiating and substituting it into the original equation. \n\n- Differentiating $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2}{{e}^{y/2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2}{{e}^{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)/2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2}{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{x}}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{x} & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{x}} \\\\ 1 & =2\\sqrt{x}.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2}{x}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2}{{e}^{y/2}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2}{{e}^{(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)/2}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2}{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{x} & =\\frac{2}{x}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true.* Therefore, the function $y=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{x}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2}{{e}^{y/2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2}{{e}^{(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)/2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2}{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{-1/2})}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2}{{x}^{-1/2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{x} & =2\\sqrt{x} \\\\ -1 & =2x\\sqrt{x}.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272207 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "181fbe61-da24-5d11-89bb-e15dc750df38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269469} {:answer/id #uuid "82aaf05c-2358-5980-b724-9f112407d897" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270398} {:answer/id #uuid "bcd83e6f-18a3-5a3a-839c-70e676840414" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270930} {:answer/id #uuid "cda477c4-b885-5340-a037-f0135038c2cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271071} {:answer/id #uuid "fc749f5e-12ad-5f00-9fd1-76a11aa02cff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271478}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bcd83e6f-18a3-5a3a-839c-70e676840414" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270930} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ab5b4d1-b93f-5fb2-8b74-1ed5e5ed886e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271755}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fbaad5c7-c732-5938-9213-ace2aa1f23df" :question/math-academy-id "q-141098" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{4y}{x},\\,x\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n Which of the following is a solution to this equation?\n\n- $y=4x$\n- $y=2{x}^{2}$\n- $y={x}^{3}$" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWe check each proposed solution by differentiating and substituting it into the original equation. \n\n- Differentiating $y=4x,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=4.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{4y}{x} \\\\ 4 & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{4(4x)}{x} \\\\ 4 & \\ne 16.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *false*. Therefore, the function $y=4x$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=2{x}^{2},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=4x.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{4y}{x} \\\\ 4x & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{4(2{x}^{2})}{x} \\\\ 4x & \\overset{?}{=}8x \\\\ -4x & \\ne 0.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=2{x}^{2}$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y={x}^{3},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=3{x}^{2}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{4y}{x} \\\\ 3{x}^{2} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{4({x}^{3})}{x} \\\\ 3{x}^{2} & \\ne 4{x}^{2} \\\\ 0 & \\ne {x}^{2}.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y={x}^{3}$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272208 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32636bf7-fcdf-57f1-b4f1-80d5f65e2bcd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269729} {:answer/id #uuid "3bc336a7-6b7e-5387-99f0-8a5cfa4145a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269814} {:answer/id #uuid "4ff4bfb8-9609-52c7-9127-adb44ed125c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269990} {:answer/id #uuid "6d8aeea4-c9b2-5447-8118-eea04ad0ab38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270243} {:answer/id #uuid "f6c5393d-d3ca-5f85-9adf-30ce831e8ce7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271434}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3bc336a7-6b7e-5387-99f0-8a5cfa4145a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269814} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a84b979-6b6b-5928-97ca-fd9627439d01" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271672}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0b4e4e14-2aab-5c49-ba69-c1255e3d6145" :question/math-academy-id "q-141099" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{2y}{x},\\,x\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n Which of the following is a solution to this equation?\n\n- $y=-\\frac{1}{x}$\n- $y=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}$\n- $y=\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}}$" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWe check each proposed solution by differentiating and substituting it into the original equation. \n\n- Differentiating $y=-\\frac{1}{x},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{2y}{x} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & \\overset{?}{=}-\\frac{2(-\\frac{1}{x})}{x} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ 0 & \\ne \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=-\\frac{1}{x}$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{2y}{x} \\\\ \\frac{2}{{x}^{3}} & \\overset{?}{=}-\\frac{2(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}{x} \\\\ \\frac{2}{{x}^{3}} & =\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true.* Therefore, the function $y=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{8}{{x}^{3}}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{2y}{x} \\\\ -\\frac{8}{{x}^{3}} & \\overset{?}{=}-\\frac{2(\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}})}{x} \\\\ -\\frac{8}{{x}^{3}} & =-\\frac{8}{{x}^{3}}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true.* Therefore, the function $y=\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}}$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272209 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28284742-46e2-5bfa-8b1f-6416a4816637" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269626} {:answer/id #uuid "462e6347-3fb0-5a17-8d03-b15563e977e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269911} {:answer/id #uuid "65a9e267-0a2f-5b55-8861-0445518b56cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270174} {:answer/id #uuid "6cfc6f3e-6a58-515d-a493-cab033a2d4f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270234} {:answer/id #uuid "91c725a6-0336-584a-a9e3-828acb6cb769" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270538}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "65a9e267-0a2f-5b55-8861-0445518b56cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270174} :answer-field/id #uuid "9becf189-0060-57b9-9574-1a0ea3d595a8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271785}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1646b369-92a0-554a-b444-f79313d419b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-141101" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{5y}{x},\\,x\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n Which of the following is a solution to this equation?\n\n- $y={x}^{5}$\n- $y={x}^{10}$\n- $y=5{x}^{4}$" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWe check each proposed solution by differentiating and substituting it into the original equation. \n\n- Differentiating $y={x}^{5},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=5{x}^{4}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{5y}{x} \\\\ 5{x}^{4} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{5({x}^{5})}{x} \\\\ 5{x}^{4} & =5{x}^{4}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y={x}^{5}$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y={x}^{10},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=10{x}^{9}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{5y}{x} \\\\ 10{x}^{9} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{5({x}^{10})}{x} \\\\ 10{x}^{9} & \\overset{?}{=}5{x}^{9} \\\\ 5{x}^{9} & \\ne 0.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y={x}^{10}$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=5{x}^{4},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=20{x}^{3}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{5y}{x} \\\\ 20{x}^{3} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{5(5{x}^{4})}{x} \\\\ 20{x}^{3} & \\overset{?}{=}25{x}^{3} \\\\ 0 & \\ne 5{x}^{3}.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=5{x}^{4}$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272210 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f9b4355-c65b-5ee8-aafc-70cf83ebfa03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269694} {:answer/id #uuid "5da76957-359f-522c-a8e9-62617e8270e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270101} {:answer/id #uuid "6a596190-3114-56c4-87f6-43ef41dd4709" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270212} {:answer/id #uuid "7a2b2a49-b8d4-50f2-bcdc-1376f690baec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270350} {:answer/id #uuid "8af21f43-d8c7-5bbf-8e55-9740b508cd0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270471}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7a2b2a49-b8d4-50f2-bcdc-1376f690baec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270350} :answer-field/id #uuid "a4f2def8-f001-5ca5-8e82-5b38b9e502c0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271808}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d8ec718b-9c17-5ad2-8af8-895d469a7090" :question/math-academy-id "q-141103" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{{e}^{3y}}.\n$$\n\n Which of the following is a solution to this equation?\n\n- $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$\n- $y=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$\n- $y=-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWe check each proposed solution by differentiating and substituting it into the original equation. \n\n- Differentiating $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{3y}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{1}{{e}^{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{1}{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{x} & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}} \\\\ {x}^{2} & =1.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{x}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{3y}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{1}{{e}^{3(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{1}{{e}^{-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{1}{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{-3})}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{1}{{x}^{-3}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{x} & ={x}^{3} \\\\ -1 & ={x}^{4}.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{2}{x}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{3y}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{1}{{e}^{3(-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{1}{{e}^{-6\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{1}{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{-6})}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{1}{{x}^{-6}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{x} & ={x}^{6} \\\\ -2 & ={x}^{7}.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348206 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "353c86aa-0e90-5fa4-9dd1-3ca8a0d3d0c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345811} {:answer/id #uuid "6fb8798b-f5c8-5735-9466-d5fd6c80b2a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346327} {:answer/id #uuid "a07420b5-8f68-5cf8-afcf-c1fc326c3fb2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346753} {:answer/id #uuid "b58f9922-eca8-59ef-b348-82def3095911" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346931} {:answer/id #uuid "e903113c-5d92-5e97-b004-620f80b18994" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347406}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "353c86aa-0e90-5fa4-9dd1-3ca8a0d3d0c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345811} :answer-field/id #uuid "6909d9cb-4327-56b2-a7a9-62f0495ae594" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347788}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "33546fc8-b13b-5d05-a639-8809e08f2462" :question/math-academy-id "q-79330" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=4y.\n$$\n\n Which of the following is a solution to this equation?\n\n- $y={e}^{-4x}$\n- $y={e}^{4x}$\n- $y=4{e}^{x}$" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWe check each proposed solution by differentiating and substituting it into the original equation. \n\n- Differentiating $y={e}^{-4x},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-4{e}^{-4x}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =4y \\\\ -4{e}^{-4x} & \\overset{?}{=}4{e}^{-4x} \\\\ 0 & \\ne 8{e}^{-4x}.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y={e}^{-4x}$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y={e}^{4x},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=4{e}^{4x}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =4y \\\\ 4{e}^{4x} & =4{e}^{4x}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y={e}^{4x}$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=4{e}^{x},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=4{e}^{x}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =4y \\\\ 4{e}^{x} & \\overset{?}{=}4(4{e}^{x}) \\\\ 4{e}^{x} & \\overset{?}{=}16{e}^{x} \\\\ 0 & \\ne 12{e}^{x}.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=4{e}^{x}$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348207 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43277281-0539-54c6-b1cd-9873ce1600ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345928} {:answer/id #uuid "45265e6e-86b7-55eb-808f-d293e54eff92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345951} {:answer/id #uuid "6658fc73-e75e-5714-8199-89d56ee053b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346244} {:answer/id #uuid "9e2c6469-2d25-5ecf-85e0-585595a5c264" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346728} {:answer/id #uuid "edd25809-6418-52d7-930f-4b2d0559c995" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347451}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6658fc73-e75e-5714-8199-89d56ee053b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346244} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae831bdf-c4ff-5afc-9212-26d4206214c9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347906}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2809b1ba-af02-5b95-b4d7-d9885f873cd9" :question/math-academy-id "q-79333" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{y}{x},\\,x\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is a solution to this equation?\n\n- $y=\\frac{7}{x}$\n- $y=-\\frac{1}{x}$\n- $y=\\frac{2}{x}$" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWe check each proposed solution by differentiating and substituting it into the original equation. \n\n- Differentiating $y=\\frac{7}{x},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{7}{{x}^{2}}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ -\\frac{7}{{x}^{2}} & \\overset{?}{=}-\\frac{(\\frac{7}{x})}{x} \\\\ -\\frac{7}{{x}^{2}} & =-\\frac{7}{{x}^{2}}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y=\\frac{7}{x}$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=-\\frac{1}{x},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & \\overset{?}{=}-\\frac{(-\\frac{1}{x})}{x} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y=-\\frac{1}{x}$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=\\frac{2}{x},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}} & \\overset{?}{=}-\\frac{(\\frac{2}{x})}{x} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}} & =-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y=\\frac{2}{x}$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to First-Order Differential Equations"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100988 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100683 :question/id #uuid "622baf41-fe35-55ee-bfc6-f1e6a2cb089a" :question/math-academy-id "e-4371" :question/problem "Find the entry $a_{23}$ of the matrix ${A},$ where \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The entry $a_{23}$ lies at the intersection of the $2$nd row and $3$rd column. Therefore, $a_{23}={\\color{blue}{-3}}.$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d783281-8364-5597-a804-a4ea0d76bec8" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048896 :topic/math-academy-id 861 :topic/title "Introduction to Matrices"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101123 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9bda8844-a8ba-550e-ad70-6a732b350a0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098074} {:answer/id #uuid "d47bfed2-903a-5b18-860a-950341cbc0c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098075} {:answer/id #uuid "f9923400-5525-55c5-9598-ae7268ed12b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098076} {:answer/id #uuid "41799c02-ef9b-5d44-ad7e-54e529360070" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No such entry" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098077} {:answer/id #uuid "72fe5296-a4e5-56e6-af38-e64f8b1e25d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098078}] :answer-field/id #uuid "b39084eb-5650-5de7-9e6f-fcc95d974ba4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098073}] :question/id #uuid "01081318-5aef-53ec-b42a-cf09d90e23dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-13488" :question/problem "Find the entry $a_{13}$ of the matrix $A$, where\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101125 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "93bf83cf-c21d-5aed-be2e-778cfc90d809" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098086} {:answer/id #uuid "eb04cc52-36c2-52c2-be8e-8cf82b15eca9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type